#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c185115e-e3ce-5503-ab5f-a3d0b1a49cfd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Function Given as a Table of Values Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "995ee2bc-32e7-52c3-80dc-414e990c310e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098680} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd47b6e-65a0-55fe-84a8-251b5428007d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098681} {:answer/id #uuid "c539c20a-8046-594a-84ee-f7f4817bdc92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098682} {:answer/id #uuid "1a103c06-551b-51c1-9bfc-8819966cdd79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{98}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098683} {:answer/id #uuid "20c11922-80fc-5319-973d-89251c21a028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a103c06-551b-51c1-9bfc-8819966cdd79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{98}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098683} :answer-field/id #uuid "871da4f1-95e8-5734-aac0-8ba943641482" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "491eead7-322b-5130-8dbc-ee448e88420a" :question/math-academy-id "q-16172" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are differentiable on an open interval containing $x=7.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are shown in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=7$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)} & ={\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}|}_{x=7} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)}{{7}^{2}\\cdot g(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{49\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x))^{′}}{({x}^{2}g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ again, and this time, we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=7} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(7)}{2\\cdot 7\\cdot g(7)+{7}^{2}\\cdot {g}^{′}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+49\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{98}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}=\\frac{1}{98}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98e43a1e-2073-593b-9b55-4f9c25c356a8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Function Given as a Table of Values Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "820e07be-f7af-5c55-845f-9376fcf32ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098686} {:answer/id #uuid "a98a572c-078b-518f-8d0d-752b729706c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098687} {:answer/id #uuid "c41d8e50-445e-5432-afc7-04fd27a889ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098688} {:answer/id #uuid "19c02e67-ec8a-5bd2-a861-50e68971733c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098690} {:answer/id #uuid "0351928c-3e28-54f2-8865-ccfc512917a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "820e07be-f7af-5c55-845f-9376fcf32ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098686} :answer-field/id #uuid "674fa78e-94ac-5ab1-b5f3-3afed06788d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7440b07-8ce3-57bb-bf61-42f3817efa6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16174" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=10.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $10$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-20$ | $-2$ | $-6$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=10$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)} & ={\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(10)+5}{20+g(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5+5}{20+(-20)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{(f(x)+5)^{′}}{(2x+g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=10$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(10)}{2+{g}^{′}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2+(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2+{g}^{′}(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{({f}^{′}(x))^{′}}{(2+{g}^{′}(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=10,$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=10} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{″}(10)}{{g}^{″}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{-6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 10}\\frac{f(x)+5}{2x+g(x)}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c185115e-e3ce-5503-ab5f-a3d0b1a49cfd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Function Given as a Table of Values Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c7e66c8-4fa9-5767-b29b-481d9fe42d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c7e66c8-4fa9-5767-b29b-481d9fe42d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507817} :answer-field/id #uuid "45f70f8d-7947-54a7-beae-a31e706e6bd8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2656d5b-6dc2-54fa-8fec-eaac03937257" :question/math-academy-id "q-337001" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are differentiable on an open interval containing $x=2.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are shown in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nUsing the values from the table, evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=2$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3} & ={\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3}|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+g(2)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(2)+2-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2+3}-\\sqrt{5}}{1+2-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5})^{′}}{(f(x)+x-3)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\frac{1+{g}^{′}(x)}{2\\sqrt{x+g(x)}}}{{f}^{′}(x)+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=2$ again, and this time, we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\frac{1+{g}^{′}(x)}{2\\sqrt{x+g(x)}}}{{f}^{′}(x)+1} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1+{g}^{′}(x)}{2\\sqrt{x+g(x)}}}{{f}^{′}(x)+1}|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1+{g}^{′}(2)}{2\\sqrt{2+g(2)}}}{{f}^{′}(2)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1+4}{2\\sqrt{5}}}{3+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+g(x)}-\\sqrt{5}}{f(x)+x-3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98e43a1e-2073-593b-9b55-4f9c25c356a8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Function Given as a Table of Values Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdf52d1c-d0cf-596a-b9d3-a36801c3cff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdf52d1c-d0cf-596a-b9d3-a36801c3cff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507883} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aaa0726-be5f-51d0-b8c1-e8e9cc273212" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "04a061b7-e5c9-583d-9ee4-5ed73b8719bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-337014" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $e$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the values from the table, evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x\\,f(x)}{\\,f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}\\,}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=1$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-xf(x)}{f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}} & ={\\frac{{e}^{x}-xf(x)}{f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-1\\cdot f(1)}{f(1)g(1)-{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-e}{(e)(1)-e} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-xf(x)}{f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({e}^{x}-xf(x))^{′}}{(f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-(f(x)+x{f}^{′}(x))}{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)+f(x){g}^{′}(x)-{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1$ again, and once more we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{e}^{x}-(f(x)+x{f}^{′}(x))}{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)+f(x){g}^{′}(x)-{e}^{x}}|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-(f(1)+1\\cdot {f}^{′}(1))}{{f}^{′}(1)g(1)+f(1){g}^{′}(1)-{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-(e+0)}{(0)(1)+(e)(1)-e} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-(f(x)+x{f}^{′}(x))}{{f}^{′}(x)g(x)+f(x){g}^{′}(x)-{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({e}^{x}-(f(x)+x{f}^{′}(x)))^{′}}{({f}^{′}(x)g(x)+f(x){g}^{′}(x)-{e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-(2{f}^{′}(x)+x{f}^{″}(x))}{{f}^{″}(x)g(x)+2{f}^{′}(x){g}^{′}(x)+f(x){g}^{″}(x)-{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute $x=1$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{e}^{x}-(2{f}^{′}(x)+x{f}^{″}(x))}{{f}^{″}(x)g(x)+2{f}^{′}(x){g}^{′}(x)+f(x){g}^{″}(x)-{e}^{x}}|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-(2{f}^{′}(1)+1\\cdot {f}^{″}(1))}{{f}^{″}(1)g(1)+2{f}^{′}(1){g}^{′}(1)+f(1){g}^{″}(1)-{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-(2(0)+0)}{(0)(1)+2(0)(1)+(e)(2)-e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{e} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-x\\,f(x)}{\\,f(x)g(x)-{e}^{x}\\,}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98e43a1e-2073-593b-9b55-4f9c25c356a8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Function Given as a Table of Values Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ad2c6b-d809-5214-a3b9-4020cbd24ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52ad2c6b-d809-5214-a3b9-4020cbd24ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507723} :answer-field/id #uuid "02cd3c44-7134-56fe-b1d4-7d91f48f8a31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8771f8a8-d420-525c-a30c-b75746b0e1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-337304" :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=1.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the values from the table, evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\,f(x)+{x}^{2}-3\\,}{\\,g(x)+{x}^{2}-4\\,}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=1$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+{x}^{2}-3}{g(x)+{x}^{2}-4} & ={\\frac{f(x)+{x}^{2}-3}{g(x)+{x}^{2}-4}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)+{1}^{2}-3}{g(1)+{1}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+1-3}{3+1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)+{x}^{2}-3}{g(x)+{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{(f(x)+{x}^{2}-3)^{′}}{(g(x)+{x}^{2}-4)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+2x}{{g}^{′}(x)+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1$ again, and once more we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+2x}{{g}^{′}(x)+2x}|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)+2(1)}{{g}^{′}(1)+2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+2}{-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)+2x}{{g}^{′}(x)+2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{({f}^{′}(x)+2x)^{′}}{({g}^{′}(x)+2x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)+2}{{g}^{″}(x)+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute $x=1$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{f}^{″}(x)+2}{{g}^{″}(x)+2}|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)+2}{{g}^{″}(1)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+2}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\,f(x)+{x}^{2}-3\\,}{\\,g(x)+{x}^{2}-4\\,}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}]}
